# Separation of Concerns for React Components

## Rule: Separate UI and Logic

React components in [src/components/](mdc:src/components/) should follow strict separation of concerns:

### ✅ Components Should Only Handle:

- JSX rendering and UI structure
- Event handlers that call hook functions
- Conditional rendering based on hook state
- Styling and accessibility attributes

### ❌ Components Should NOT Handle:

- State management (use custom hooks)
- API calls or data fetching
- Complex business logic
- Side effects (useEffect)
- Data transformations

### Pattern to Follow:

```tsx
// ✅ Good - UI Component
import { useMyFeature } from "@/hooks/use-my-feature";

export function MyComponent({ prop1, prop2 }: MyComponentProps) {
  const { data, loading, error, handleAction, isSelected } = useMyFeature({
    prop1,
    prop2,
  });

  if (loading) return <LoadingSpinner />;
  if (error) return <ErrorMessage error={error} />;

  return (
    <div className="my-component">
      {data.map((item) => (
        <button key={item.id} onClick={() => handleAction(item)}>
          {item.name}
        </button>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// ✅ Good - Logic Hook
export function useMyFeature({ prop1, prop2 }: UseMyFeatureProps) {
  const [state, setState] = useState(initialState);

  const query = useQuery({
    queryKey: ["my-feature", prop1],
    queryFn: () => fetchData(prop1),
  });

  const handleAction = useCallback((item: Item) => {
    // Business logic here
    setState((prevState) => updateState(prevState, item));
  }, []);

  const isSelected = useCallback(
    (id: string) => {
      return state.selectedIds.includes(id);
    },
    [state.selectedIds]
  );

  return {
    data: query.data || [],
    loading: query.isLoading,
    error: query.error,
    handleAction,
    isSelected,
  };
}
```

### Custom Hook Naming Convention:

- Use `use-` prefix followed by feature name
- Store in [src/hooks/](mdc:src/hooks/) directory
- Export with descriptive TypeScript interfaces

### Benefits:

- Better testability (test logic separately from UI)
- Reusable logic across components
- Cleaner, more readable components
- Easier maintenance and debugging
